    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">Here&#8217;s a number I have never been able to get out of my head: <strong style="font-weight:700;">95%</strong>.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">That is the false positive rate on cross-border sanctions screening at any major bank. Twenty-five thousand alerts per day. Fifteen to forty-five minutes per investigation. Ninety-five percent of them clearing to nothing. Every analyst I have worked with over the last three decades knows the rhythm: triage, dismiss, triage, dismiss, escalate one in a hundred, repeat. We built an entire compliance industry around the assumption that messy address data is simply a condition of the job.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;"><strong style="font-weight:700;">From 15 November 2026, that assumption breaks.</strong></p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 20px 0;">Every cross-border and SEPA payment must carry its address in structured ISO 20022 form. The screening engines do not change. The watchlists do not change. The regulators do not change. The data changes &#8212; and four FATF compliance domains transform as a result. Not by 5% or 10%. Architecturally, in ways that were not previously available at any price.</p>

    <!-- Callout: The Distinction -->
