    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">Sanctions false positives have two specific generators, and structured addresses neutralise both in a single step.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">The first is geographic name collision: a payment to <strong style="font-weight:700;">169 Cuba Street, Wellington, New Zealand</strong> hits screening as a Cuba match because the engine cannot tell a street name from a country. The second is name-address confusion: a payment to <strong style="font-weight:700;">Mohammed Ali Road, Mumbai</strong> generates a person match because the engine cannot distinguish a road from a name in unstructured text.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 20px 0;">Structured fields end both. Cuba sits in a street field. Mohammed Ali sits in a street field. The comparison is like-for-like. The alert never fires.</p>

