    <h2 style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:900;font-size:22px;line-height:1.25;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 20px 0;letter-spacing:-0.3px;">Four FATF Recommendations. One Upstream Fix.</h2>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 20px 0;">The compliance dividend is not four separate benefits from four separate investments. Sanctions screening, AML monitoring, KYC verification, and Travel Rule integrity have all been bottlenecked by the same data problem. One change in data structure releases all four simultaneously.</p>

    <!-- FATF Rec 1 -->
    <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 10px 0;">
    <tr>
      <td style="background:#F0F4F9;border:1px solid #D0DAEB;border-radius:6px;padding:16px 20px;">
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#43ADC1;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.8px;margin:0 0 6px 0;">FATF Recommendation 1 &#8212; AML Risk Scoring</p>
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#232F3E;margin:0;"><strong style="font-weight:700;">Geographic risk scoring becomes deterministic at message receipt.</strong> Today&#8217;s transaction monitor derives geography probabilistically from unstructured text, then applies a risk band to a guess. Structured fields produce an exact answer: Frankfurt scores standard; Erbil triggers enhanced due diligence; a Dubai Free Trade Zone postcode raises a trade-based money laundering flag. No parsing, no inference.</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    <!-- FATF Rec 10 -->
    <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 10px 0;">
    <tr>
      <td style="background:#F0F4F9;border:1px solid #D0DAEB;border-radius:6px;padding:16px 20px;">
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#43ADC1;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.8px;margin:0 0 6px 0;">FATF Recommendation 10 &#8212; KYC / Customer Due Diligence</p>
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#232F3E;margin:0;"><strong style="font-weight:700;">KYC shifts from document collection to data verification.</strong> Structured address fields are validated against postal authority reference data across 246 countries at onboarding. The question changes from &#8220;did we collect this?&#8221; to &#8220;does this resolve?&#8221; &#8212; which is the substantive standard FATF Recommendation 10 has always implied, and which institutions have been unable to meet because unstructured data cannot be deterministically resolved.</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    <!-- FATF Rec 16 -->
    <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 10px 0;">
    <tr>
      <td style="background:#F0F4F9;border:1px solid #D0DAEB;border-radius:6px;padding:16px 20px;">
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#43ADC1;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.8px;margin:0 0 6px 0;">FATF Recommendation 16 &#8212; Travel Rule / Correspondent Chain</p>
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#232F3E;margin:0;"><strong style="font-weight:700;">Confirmation of Payee becomes a three-way deterministic join:</strong> beneficiary name &#215; IBAN &#215; structured address record. Today, the 1970s-era 4&#215;35 character limit strips postcode and city by hop four &#8212; the institution closest to the sanctions risk receives the worst data. ISO 20022 MX fields are unbounded; the full structured address survives every hop intact, and screening is effective across the entire correspondent chain.</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    <!-- FATF Rec 24/25 -->
    <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 10px 0;">
    <tr>
      <td style="background:#F0F4F9;border:1px solid #D0DAEB;border-radius:6px;padding:16px 20px;">
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#43ADC1;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.8px;margin:0 0 6px 0;">FATF Recommendations 24/25 &#8212; Beneficial Ownership</p>
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#232F3E;margin:0;"><strong style="font-weight:700;">Shell company networks become visible through address clustering.</strong> When addresses are normalised against postal reference data, forty-seven entity registrations at one canonical address surface in the dataset. Today the same registrations look like forty-seven different addresses &#8212; variant formatting, different abbreviations, different building number styles. The shell company network has always been there in the data. The data structure has been the thing hiding it.</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
